      "metadata": {
        "id": "jjLFaP82sGE_"
      },
      "source": [
        "## **3.2 Hyperparameter Tuning: Optimal Topic Selection (k)**\n",
        "Perplexity was use to scores and qualitative inspection to determine the best number of topics for the business analysis."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4IJP3kyHsO6V",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 546
        },
        "outputId": "6eb3e361-37da-40b3-9c50-cbb3581242a8"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "k=3 -> perplexity=992.2\n",
            "k=4 -> perplexity=1095.4\n",
            "k=5 -> perplexity=1203.2\n",
            "k=6 -> perplexity=1259.3\n",
            "k=7 -> perplexity=1269.7\n",
            "k=8 -> perplexity=1351.0\n",
            "k=9 -> perplexity=1404.5\n",
            "k=10 -> perplexity=1450.8\n"
          ]
        },
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 700x400 with 1 Axes>"
            ],
            "image/png": "\n"
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.decomposition import LatentDirichletAllocation\n",
        "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "dtm_train, dtm_test = train_test_split(dtm, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)\n",
        "\n",
        "topic_range = [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10]\n",
        "perplexities = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for k in topic_range:\n",
        "    lda_tmp = LatentDirichletAllocation(\n",
        "        n_components=k, random_state=42, learning_method='online', max_iter=10, n_jobs=-1\n",
        "    )\n",
        "    lda_tmp.fit(dtm_train)\n",
        "    perplexities.append(lda_tmp.perplexity(dtm_test))\n",
        "    print(f\"k={k} -> perplexity={perplexities[-1]:.1f}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(7,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(topic_range, perplexities, marker='o')\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Number of Topics (k)\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Perplexity (lower = better fit)\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Perplexity vs Number of Topics\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "o9O9LzatsWj7"
      },
      "source": [
        "Justification: Perplexity rises with k here (common for review datasets with a lot of vocabulary overlap between topics), so the curve alone favours the smallest k. But the rate of increase flattens noticeably between k=5 and k=6 (+43, then +35) before rising more steeply again from k=7 onward — a mild \"elbow.\" Combined with manual inspection, k=6 gives topics that are each clearly interpretable and map onto distinct, actionable business themes (fewer topics merged unrelated themes together; more topics started duplicating each other). So k=6 was chosen as the balance between statistical fit and interpretability."
      ]
    },
